// loft#2267 — A REFERENCE ACROSS A METHOD THAT REMOVES FROM ITS CONTAINER IS REFUSED (`@FR-B-Ref-Reshape`, `@FR-B-Disturb`).  A method is a callee like any free function: `b.drop_first()` removes the element `c` names, and the next element moves into its place.  The disturbance summary read only `n_` functions, so the write through `c` landed on the element that moved in.
//
// @falsified-at: 23bc61e47 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// WITNESS: main compiled the program and ran the write through the stale reference.
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostic.
// ARMED: no instrument.
// HOLDS: the free-function spelling was refused already; the plain bind is copied out —
//   `rules/binding/B-Disturb/2267-a-clear-or-a-method-ends-the-views-into-a-vector.loft`.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot call `drop_first` while `c` references a place inside `b`

struct S { n: integer }
struct Bag { items: vector<S> }

fn drop_first(self: Bag) { self.items.remove(0); }

fn main() {
  b = Bag { items: [S { n: 1 }, S { n: 2 }] };
  c = &b.items[0];
  b.drop_first();
  c.n = 9;
  println("{b.items[0].n} {len(b.items)}");
}
